# Example 65: Structured JSON Logging. (co-14)
#
# Unstructured logs ("started thing") are easy to read and hard to query.
# STRUCTURED logs (one JSON object per line, with a timestamp + level + fields)
# are machine-parseable: filter, count, and alert on fields, not grep on prose.
# This Caddyfile switches the proxy log to JSON and shows the app-side shape.

# --- Proxy side: Caddy logs every request as one JSON line -------------------
# Save as /etc/caddy/Caddyfile (extends Example 12's log block).
myapp.example.com {
  reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000
  log {
    output file /var/log/caddy/access.log
    format json  # => co-14: one JSON object per request (was 'console' in Example 12)
  }
}

# --- App side: the app should log JSON too (Python example) ------------------
# In the app, emit one JSON line per event:
#   import json, time, sys
#   def log(level, msg, **fields):
#       sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({"ts": time.time(), "level": level, "msg": msg, **fields}) + "\n")
#   log("info", "request", path="/items", status=200, ms=12)
# => co-14: now 'journalctl -u myapp' AND the proxy log both speak JSON
# --- Why JSON (co-14) --------------------------------------------------------
# A query like "count of 5xx in the last hour" is a jq filter on JSON, not a
# fragile grep on free text -- and a log shipper (Example 66) can index the
# fields directly.
# --- Verify ------------------------------------------------------------------
#   tail -n 1 /var/log/caddy/access.log | jq .   # => a parsed JSON object, one request
# [verify] the log line is valid JSON with named fields (co-14 proof).